There are a couple other things that might be helpful with regard to the DTR functionality of the startup.  Namely, if you want the console output to begin right away without having to hit a key on the console, you need to set a couple other flow control settings:
```
uart_line_ctrl_set(dev, UART_LINE_CTRL_DCD, 1);
uart_line_ctrl_set(dev, UART_LINE_CTRL_DSR, 1);
```

The problem that we need to decide on is:

- We can enable USB when the console is initialized (i.e. what is being done now) and then provide the application with a way of adding its own callback **and** finding out the current USB state (this is not feasible for all USB drivers). This allows early print statements to go out during the boot process.

- We remove the auto-initialization of USB in the console and require the application to call `usb_enable()`. This will cause some early boot messages . The other issue is waiting for DTR. One possibility would be:

```
main()
{
  /* anything sent to the console so far has been dropped */
   int res = usb_enable(callback);
   /* console is enabled now */
   while(!dtr) {
       uart_line_ctrl_get(uart_console_dev, UART_LINE_CTRL_DTR, &dtr);
       k_msleep(100);
  }
  /* console is ready, messages won't be dropped */
}
```

This makes sense because today, the [USB console](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/master/drivers/console/uart_console.c#L633) is initialized at the [application runlevel](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/a2d4292a96e0e3f710374b6da6d3d5060bccc905/kernel/init.c#L246), which actually happens just before calling `main()`. So the advantage of having auto-initialization is minimal today.

This is our preferred solution at this point. For this to work we need to make sure that the UART driver in `cdc_acm.c` can handle calls to `uart_poll_out()` and `uart_fifo_fill()` before USB is enabled, dropping the characters (or storing them in a ring buffer if we really want to).

Also don't lose sight of [the other places](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues/27071#issuecomment-662679087) that might be breaking the API.
```
drivers/console/uart_console.c: ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/net/lib/openthread/platform/uart.c:      int ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/tracing/tracing_backend_usb.c:   ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/usb/class/netusb/netusb.c:       ret = usb_enable(NULL);
```

>Also don't lose sight of the other places that might be breaking the API.
drivers/console/uart_console.c: ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/net/lib/openthread/platform/uart.c:      int ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/tracing/tracing_backend_usb.c:   ret = usb_enable(NULL);
subsys/usb/class/netusb/netusb.c:       ret = usb_enable(NULL);

I put some work towards that and I can't say I like that. For `uart_console.c` and `tracing_backend_usb.c` changes are acceptable but for `netusb.c` putting `usb_enable` to application (main) means forcing main to execute a lot of dependent code I personally don't like. Zperf sample is using those and calling `usb_enable` from application main means that some additional code must be added (I am not a fan of that) just because the user wants USB. I enclose a wip [branch](https://github.com/emob-nordic/zephyr/tree/usb_console_enable) as reference.

